{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_0","type":"agent_message","text":"Under Irish UCITS ETF rules, a secondary-market investor must be allowed to redeem directly with the ETF when its exchange price **significantly diverges from NAV**—for example, during market disruption caused by the absence of a market maker.\n\nProtections include:\n- **Notification:** information must be communicated to the regulated market that the ETF is open for direct redemptions.\n- **Process:** the prospectus must explain how secondary-market investors can exercise this exceptional right.\n- **Costs:** potential costs must be disclosed in the prospectus and must not be excessive. Redemption is not necessarily free.\n\nOrdinarily, investors buy and sell on-exchange through brokers at market prices, bearing spreads and dealing charges; authorised participants generally handle direct creations and redemptions.\n\n**Worth knowing:** This is a safeguard against significant price/NAV divergence, not an unconditional right for every exchange investor to redeem directly whenever desired."}}
